  "plan": {
    "goal": "Build an alveolar-capillary barrier chip perfused at the very low physiological shear of the lung alveolus (~0.03 Pa).",
    "rationale": "The lung alveolus experiences the lowest physiological shear in the body (~0.03 Pa, 0.3 dyn/cm²). The A549 registry confirms an alveolar shear range of 0.02–0.05 Pa, so 0.03 Pa is a defensible mid-range target. A wide, shallow channel (1000 µm × 100 µm) mimics the broad, thin alveolar-capillary interface and, at 3 µL/min, produces exactly 0.03 Pa wall shear (verified). Reynolds ≈ 0.09 confirms laminar flow; pressure drop ≈ 12 Pa is negligible for any syringe/peristaltic pump; residence time ≈ 40 s. Oxygen supply exceeds demand ~167×, so the cell layer is not flow-limited for O2. For a barrier model, seed A549 (alveolar epithelium) on the apical side and HUVEC (endothelium) on the basal side of a porous membrane; shear is applied to the vascular (HUVEC) channel.",
